## ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
Inspect all three screenshots and their HTML before deciding. Reject concrete defects that affect reading, use, or the source's meaningful layout relationships:
- Overlap, clipping, unreadable contrast, content hidden behind images, or page-wide horizontal overflow. Normal vertical scrolling and a taller page are expected with accessible text.
- Missing, duplicated or changed content; broken/distorted images; incorrect DOM or visual reading order; unusable or detached learner controls. Do not infer missing content from unrelated regions of the original.
- Substantially wrong hierarchy, alignment or column structure, an image reduced to a thumbnail or stretched across a column it only partly occupied, or an image/sidebar separated from the text it belongs beside. At desktop preserve these relationships; at tablet preserve them where legible at the fixed type scale; stack when necessary at mobile.
- A substantial artificial gap interrupting continuous reading, such as paragraphs pushed below an entire tall sidebar or isolated grid rows. Ordinary whitespace around a shorter column, a heading or an illustration is acceptable.

Accept reasonable spacing, color/background and image-size differences, full-width section adaptation, and responsive reflow. Do not redesign an acceptable page to match print pixels or your preferred aesthetic.
- The large `adt-*` type scale is intentional. Never reduce it to make the print layout fit.
- Keep text `sr-only` only when those same words are legible in the displayed image; that accessible transcription is not missing content.
- Raw LaTeX in math elements is expected: packaging converts it to MathML. Do not reject raw notation or wrap/alter formula text to repair a source-string appearance. Still fix clipping or overlap of surrounding real content.
- Screenshots cannot demonstrate clicking, answer checking or drag behavior. Preserve existing activity hooks; do not rebuild controls merely because a still image cannot show their interaction.

## REPAIR STRATEGY
Fix the cause and check the consequences across all three widths before returning HTML. Preserve parts that already work.
- Resolve overlap through normal flow and adequate space. For retained overlays use explicit stacking (`z-10` or higher for text) and keep them legible; stack overlays at mobile when needed.
- Size images relative to their source text column (usually `w-1/4` through `w-3/4`, `w-full` for full-column images), retain aspect ratio with `h-auto`, and keep captions/controls attached. Do not shrink content images below a quarter column to force a fit.
- For sidebar gaps, choose a layout compatible with saved DOM order. A sidebar preceding the prose can float at that exact position while the following text wraps beside it; do not force it above the text to match a simplistic top-to-bottom scan. If it follows earlier prose, wrap only the subsequent text. Do not reorder nodes or isolate paragraphs in separate grid rows.
- When the source stacks consecutive sidebar boxes on the right, a horizontal card row above the prose is a layout defect. Keep those boxes in one right-floating wrapper with a vertical stack, preserving their internal order; do not float each box independently. Put `flow-root` on the common parent of the float and prose, and keep following paragraphs in normal block flow. Retain this relationship at desktop and tablet while readable, stack in DOM order on mobile, and check that later prose resumes below the panels without a gap. Do not gather sidebar nodes across intervening content.
- For narrow tables, allocate space to complete labels and controls; avoid breaking words into unreadable single letters or rotating an entire table to fix one header. Preserve table semantics and fixed font sizes.
- Keep banner text aligned with the body's centered reading column; backgrounds may span the viewport. Avoid fixed heights that clip wrapped text.

Copy unaffected HTML unchanged. Limit edits to the class attributes and wrappers needed for the observed defect; do not expand existing shared styles onto every table cell or restyle borders while repairing a header.

Readable multi-line table headers are acceptable, even when a long word wraps inside the word. Reject only overlap, clipping or labels that cannot be read reliably, not recognizable wrapped labels.

Apply repair utilities directly to the affected elements (for example each table header), not through a new ancestor selector. HTML-escaped selector characters such as `&amp;` in arbitrary Tailwind variants can leave a seemingly valid repair visually ineffective. Preserve existing unrelated classes.

